\title{Approximating Solutions of ODEs}
\date{2023-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{We look at 2 problems:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{(i)} How to construct ‘local’ asymptotic solutions near an ODE’s irregular singular points.}
\li{\strong{(ii)} How to construct ‘global’ solutions to ODEs with a small/large parameter.}
}

\p{\strong{Review of 2nd order ODEs}}

\p{Consider}

##{y'' + p(x)y' + q(x)y = 0 \qquad (\ast)}

\p{with #{p, q} known functions.}

\ul{
\li{Often we want to construct ‘local’ solutions around some point #{x_0}.}
\li{If #{p} and #{q} are analytic at #{x_0}, then #{x_0} is an \em{ordinary point}, and we are guaranteed 2 series solutions of (#{\ast}) of the form #{y = \sum_{n=0}^\infty a_n(x - x_0)^n}.}
\li{If #{p} and #{q} are \em{not} analytic at #{x_0}, but #{(x - x_0)p} and #{(x - x_0)^2q} are, then #{x_0} is a \em{regular singular point} and we are guaranteed one solution of the form #{y = \sum_{n=0}^\infty b_n(x - x_0)^{n + \sigma}}.}
\li{Otherwise #{x_0} is an \em{irregular singular point}.}
\li{\p{We can categorise #{\infty} similarly by setting #{t = 1/x} and checking #{t = 0}. Let #{Y(t) = y(x)}; then}
##{\frac{dy}{dx} = -t^2\frac{dY}{dt}, \qquad \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = t^4\frac{d^2Y}{dt^2} + 2t^3\frac{dY}{dt},}
\p{so (#{\ast}) becomes #{Y'' + \tilde{p}(t)Y' + \tilde{q}(t)Y = 0} with}
##{\tilde{p} = \frac{2}{t} - \frac{p(1/t)}{t^2}, \qquad \tilde{q} = \frac{q(1/t)}{t^4},}
\p{and then categorise #{t = 0} (i.e. #{x \to \infty}).}}
}

\transclude{AM10}

\p{ODEs like (#{\ast}) can be reduced to the canonical form #{u'' = Q(x)u}. Let #{y = u(x)v(x)} and put into (#{\ast}):}

##{vu'' + (2v' + pv)u' + (v'' + pv' + qv)u = 0.}

\p{Choose #{v} such that #{2v' + pv = 0 \Rightarrow v = e^{-\frac{1}{2}\int p\,dx}}. Then}

##{u'' = \underbrace{\Big( -\frac{v'' + pv' + qv}{v} \Big)}_{Q(x)}u.}

\p{\strong{Irregular Singular Points & the Liouville–Green Method}}

\p{Consider}

##{y'' - Q(x)y = 0 \qquad (\ast\ast)}

\p{and #{x = x_0} is an irregular singular point (so #{Q} blows up badly there). How about this problem? #{y'' = \frac{1}{x^4}y}: #{x = 0} is an irregular singular point, but #{y = Axe^{1/x} + Bxe^{-1/x}}. [\strong{Exercise:} show this is true; hint: let #{t = 1/x}.]}

\p{Noting the poles in the exponential, we try #{y = e^{S(x)}} and stick into (#{\ast\ast}): #{y' = e^SS'}, #{y'' = e^SS'' + e^S(S')^2}, so}

##{(\ast\ast) \implies S'' + (S')^2 = Q(x) \quad \text{(Riccati equation)}.}

\p{Try #{S \sim S_0 + S_1 + S_2 + \cdots}, where the #{S_i} form an asymptotic sequence as #{x \to x_0}. To leading order, #{S_0'' + (S_0')^2 \sim Q}. But assume #{S_0'' \ll (S_0')^2} as #{x \to x_0} (to be checked later). Then}

##{(S_0')^2 \sim Q \implies S_0 \sim \pm\int \sqrt{Q}\,dx.}

\p{In fact, we set #{S_0 = \pm\int \sqrt{Q}\,dx}. Consistency check for the assumption: #{S_0'' = \pm\frac{1}{2}\frac{Q'}{\sqrt{Q}} \ll Q = (S_0')^2}. (To see this, suppose #{Q \propto (x - x_0)^{-b}}, #{b > 2}, near #{x = x_0}. \strong{Exercise:} do in detail.)}

\p{To get #{S_1}, we have #{(S_0 + S_1)'' + (S_0' + S_1')^2 \sim Q}, so}

##{S_1'' + (S_1')^2 + 2S_0'S_1' \sim -S_0'' = \mp\frac{Q'}{2\sqrt{Q}}.}

\p{Now assume #{S_1 \ll S_0}, #{S_1' \ll S_0'} and #{S_1'' \ll 2S_0'S_1'} (to be checked later). Then}

##{2S_0'S_1' \sim \mp\frac{Q'}{2\sqrt{Q}} \implies S_1' \sim -\frac{1}{4}\frac{Q'}{Q},}

\p{so set #{S_1 = -\frac{1}{4}\log Q}. [\strong{Exercise:} show the 3 assumptions are valid.] So the general asymptotic solution to (#{\ast\ast}) looks like}

##{y \sim AQ^{-1/4}e^{\int \sqrt{Q}\,dx} + BQ^{-1/4}e^{-\int \sqrt{Q}\,dx} \quad \text{as } x \to x_0.}

\p{This is the \em{Liouville–Green approximation}.}

\transclude{AM11}

\p{\strong{ODEs with a Small/Large Parameter}}

\p{We consider #{y'' + a(\varepsilon, x)y' + b(\varepsilon, x)y = 0}, or equivalently #{y'' = Q(\varepsilon, x)y}, where #{0 < \varepsilon \ll 1} and we have boundary conditions (or initial conditions). Often solutions exhibit variations on 2 different scales: a short/fast scale and a long/slow scale, sometimes at different regions of the domain.}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(a)} \em{Regular vs. singular asymptotics.}}
\ul{
\li{\p{Consider #{y'' + 2\varepsilon y' + y = 0} as #{\varepsilon \to 0}.}
\ul{
\li{The “zeroth order” solution (i.e. when #{\varepsilon = 0}): #{y = Ae^{ix} + Be^{-ix}}.}
\li{The general solution: #{y = Ae^{\lambda_1x} + Be^{\lambda_2x}}, #{\lambda_{1,2} = -\varepsilon \pm i\sqrt{1 - \varepsilon^2} = \pm i - \varepsilon \mp \frac{i}{2}\varepsilon^2 + \cdots}.}
}
\p{The #{\varepsilon = 0} solution matches smoothly to the #{\varepsilon \to 0} limit. This is called a \em{regular asymptotic solution}.}}
\li{\p{Consider #{\varepsilon^2y'' + y = 0} as #{\varepsilon \to 0}.}
\ul{
\li{Zeroth order solution: #{y = 0} (but check the boundary conditions!).}
\li{General solution: #{y = A\cos(\frac{x}{\varepsilon}) + B\sin(\frac{x}{\varepsilon})}.}
}
\p{This doesn’t match smoothly to the #{\varepsilon = 0} solution. This is a \em{singular perturbation problem}.}}
}
\p{Generally when #{\varepsilon} multiplies the highest derivative in the ODE, you have a singular perturbation problem. Usually, asymptotic approaches are the only way forward.}}
\li{\p{\strong{(b)} \em{Asymptotic matching.} The principle of asymptotic matching:}
\ul{
\li{Suppose the domain is broken into two \em{overlapping} regions.}
\li{Suppose we have an asymptotic approximation on each region/interval.}
\li{Matching requires that the asymptotic approximations have the same functional form on the \em{overlapping region}.}
}}
}

\p{\strong{The WKBJ Method}}

\p{Consider the BVP}

##{\varepsilon^2y'' = q(x)y \qquad (\oplus)}

\ul{
\li{for #{\varepsilon} small, usually on an infinite domain and with boundary conditions #{y \to 0} as #{x \to \pm\infty};}
\li{#{q} is a known function without singularities.}
}

\p{(#{q} can be thought of as a potential, and thus this parallels the motion of a particle.)}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(a)} A point #{x = a} is a \em{turning point} if #{q(a) = 0}.}
\ul{
\li{Usually a solution undergoes a qualitative shift in its behaviour as it goes through #{x = a}.}
\li{‘Wavelike’ behaviour for #{q < 0}.}
\li{Exponentially growing/decaying behaviour for #{q > 0}.}
}
\p{E.g. consider (i) #{y'' = \operatorname{sgn}(x)y} with #{y \to 0} as #{x \to \infty} (\strong{exercise:} solve this explicitly); (ii) #{y'' = xy}.}
